---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import tm from "../data/dna-23andme.json";
import corpus from "../data/dna-match-corpus.json";
import probes from "../data/dna-match-probes.json";
import ac from "../data/dna-autoclusters.json";
const C = tm.ancestry_composition.breakdown;
const bp = tm.dna_relatives.ancestor_birthplace_census.countries;
const bpList = Object.entries(bp).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
const probeRows = probes.filter((r) => r.kind === "probe");
const controlRows = probes.filter((r) => r.kind === "control");
const em = (t) => t.replace(/\*\*\* (.+?) \*\*\*/g, "<strong>$1</strong>");
---
<Base title="The DNA"
      eyebrow="A third leg under the Argentine origin — and a better question"
      dek="A granddaughter of Pablo Armando has tested at MyHeritage. One result confirms outright what until a fortnight ago rested on a single passport. The other unsettles an assumption this archive had been making without noticing.">

<section class="blk">
  <div class="panel" style="margin:1.4rem 0">
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>On privacy, first.</strong> The test belongs to a <strong>living
      person</strong>, and this archive omits the living. She is not named on this page and
      <strong>her ethnicity profile is not published</strong>. Three figures appear below, and only
      because they are evidence about <strong>Pablo Armando, who died in 1950</strong>. The rest is held
      privately. <a href={u("/method/")}>Method &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Her position in the descent is what makes any of it readable: <strong>she is one quarter Pablo
      Armando</strong>. Her other three grandparents are <strong>Mary Septima Taylor</strong> &mdash;
      English, of Devon and Suffolk &mdash; and <strong>two Booyzens</strong>, Cape Afrikaner.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What it settles</h2>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:20px 0">
    <div class="lab">Genetic group &middot; confidence: high</div>
    <p class="lede" style="margin:10px 0 0">Argentina (Corrientes and Buenos Aires), Uruguay and
      Paraguay</p>
    <p class="cap" style="margin-top:8px">Group 6330 &middot; built from <strong>178 DNA kits</strong>,
      61 of them linked to family trees</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>A genetic group is not an ethnicity percentage. It is a cluster of people who share actual
      segments of chromosome and whose documented trees converge on one region. This one converges on the
      <strong>Río de la Plata</strong>.</p>
    <p><strong>It can only have come from him.</strong> English Devon and Cape Afrikaner do not produce a
      Río de la Plata cluster. Of her four grandparents, exactly one was born in Argentina.</p>
    <p>So the Argentine origin now stands on <strong>three independent legs</strong>: his own
      <a href={u("/passport/")}>1940 passport</a>; the <a href={u("/enrolment-card/")}>1908 army enrolment
      card</a> found in a drawer in Buenos Aires; and a chromosome. A fortnight ago it stood on one.</p>
    <p><strong>And the group&rsquo;s top places for 1900&ndash;1950 name <em>Santa Fe</em>.</strong> Not
      merely Argentina, and not merely Buenos Aires &mdash; Santa Fe, by name, alongside Buenos Aires,
      Corrientes and Entre Ríos. That is the first time anything outside this family&rsquo;s own paperwork
      has independently pointed at the province the passport and the card both give.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What it questions</h2>
  <p class="dek">Here the result is more interesting than comfortable.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel"><div class="nm">0.0%</div>
      <div class="txt">Iberian <strong>at MyHeritage</strong> &mdash; none at all.
        <br /><span class="cap">23andMe, reading the same saliva, finds <strong>2.1%</strong>.
        See below.</span></div></div>
    <div class="panel"><div class="nm">11.1%</div>
      <div class="txt">Italian <strong>at MyHeritage</strong> &mdash; North 6.1, South 3.6, Sardinian 1.4.
        <br /><span class="cap">23andMe finds <strong>8.0%</strong>, and calls it Sicilian.</span></div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="note" style="margin-bottom:18px"><strong>This paragraph was written on one company&rsquo;s
      reading and has been corrected.</strong> On <strong>16 September 2026</strong> the same DNA was read
      at 23andMe, and it returns <strong>2.1% Spanish &amp; Portuguese</strong> where MyHeritage returns
      nothing. The sentence below is kept, struck through in substance rather than deleted, because the
      hypothesis it launched is still on this page and a reader is owed the correction beside the claim.
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>The corrections &rarr;</a></div>
    <p><strong>There is no Spanish component whatever</strong> &mdash; <em>at MyHeritage</em>. If Pablo
      Armando had been the Spanish-descended criollo this archive has quietly assumed, a granddaughter
      should carry something in the order of <strong>25% Iberian</strong>. MyHeritage gives her none;
      23andMe gives her 2.1%, which is <em>also</em> far short of 25%, so the shape of the argument
      survives even though the flat zero does not.</p>
    <p>What she does carry is <strong>Italian</strong> &mdash; and her other three grandparents are
      English and Afrikaner, so it has nowhere else to come from. At one quarter, <strong>11.1% in her
      implies roughly 44% in him: about one Italian parent.</strong></p>
    <p>And at MyHeritage the split is <strong>northern-dominant</strong>, 6.1% against 3.6% &mdash; which
      this archive read as the signature of the Ligurian and Piedmontese emigration that made Rosario the
      most Italian city in Argentina. <strong>23andMe points the other way and names Sicily.</strong>
      Two panels agree that the Italian is there and disagree about which end of Italy it came from, so
      <strong>the northern reading is withdrawn as a DNA argument</strong>. The Ligurian names in the
      Rosario register are still in the Rosario register; they were never a DNA claim.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Why that would explain rather a lot</h2>
  <p class="dek">If <strong>Juan Carlos or María Lerena was an Italian immigrant</strong>, four stubborn
    nulls stop being mysterious.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:20rem">The null</th><th>What an Italian family would explain</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>No Lerena marriage at Rosario, 1867&ndash;1885</strong><br />
          <span class="cap">read at full resolution, and clean</span></td>
          <td>A couple who married <strong>in Italy before emigrating</strong> would not be in that book
            at all.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>The register around his birth</strong><br />
          <span class="cap">two months read act by act</span></td>
          <td>It is <strong>full of Ligurian and Piedmontese names</strong> &mdash; Piaggio, Ricotti,
            Savoni, Bianchi, Ferrari, Daneri, Scorzetti, Busaglia, Forchelli, Balerini, Peduci, Louraschi,
            Ranieri, Figallo, Comazzi, Tavella, Angeli. That is the world he was born into.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>His death was registered LERINA</strong><br />
          <span class="cap">treated here as a clerk&rsquo;s slip</span></td>
          <td>CEMLA records <strong>LERINA as an <em>Italian</em> surname</strong> &mdash; Laurensa
            Lerina, 59, and Savina Lerina, 55, both <em>Italiana</em>, arriving from <strong>Genova</strong>
            in 1889. If the family were North Italian, <strong>Lerina may be nearer the original than
            Lerena</strong>, and the &ldquo;error&rdquo; may be the one document that got it right.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Cajón 985 filed the name by sound</strong></td>
          <td>Lera, Lorena, Serena, Perena, all interfiled &mdash; a clerk hearing a foreign name.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px"><strong>None of this is proved.</strong> It is a hypothesis
    with unusual explanatory reach, and it is written down as one rather than slipped in as a finding.
    <a href={u("/hypotheses/")}>The hypotheses &rarr;</a></div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The other groups &mdash; and they weaken the Italian case</h2>
  <p class="dek">Same day. The slider was moved to low confidence and the count went from three of eight
    to <strong>eight of eight</strong>. The result argues against the hypothesis above, and it is recorded
    here rather than left in a drawer.</p>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:16px">
    <p><strong>A technical point first:</strong> the confidence slider governs the <em>genetic groups</em>
      only. The ethnicity percentages are identical in both views &mdash; the same thirteen values, the
      same figures. Nothing above changes.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:20px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:24rem">Five of the eight, legible</th><th>Whose it is</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>South Africa</td><td>the Booyzen half</td></tr>
        <tr><td>South Africa and Zimbabwe</td><td>the family&rsquo;s spread into the Transvaal and beyond</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Argentina (Corrientes and Buenos Aires), Uruguay and Paraguay</strong></td>
          <td><strong>can only be Pablo Armando</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>England</td><td>Mary Septima Taylor</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ireland (Cork, Kerry and Limerick) and England</td><td>Mary Septima Taylor, or the Cape</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">Three remain below the fold and have not been seen.</p>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <strong>No Italian genetic group appears among the five.</strong> That is evidence
    <em>against</em> the Italian reading, and it arrived within an hour of the hypothesis being written
    down.
    <br /><br />
    It does not kill it &mdash; a genetic group needs enough tested people from a region with documented
    trees, and an Italian great-grandparent&rsquo;s cluster can fail to form. But <strong>the easiest
    confirmation was available and did not appear</strong>, and the case is weaker for it than it was
    this morning.
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p><strong>The asymmetry is worth stating plainly.</strong> The Río de la Plata group is a
      <em>positive</em> result: a cluster formed, at high confidence, from 178 kits. The Italian claim
      rests on a <em>negative</em> &mdash; an absence of Iberian percentage &mdash; plus arithmetic on an
      11.1% figure. Those are not the same class of evidence, and this archive should not treat them as
      though they were.</p>
    <p><strong>And one thing the eight groups do confirm.</strong> Every group except the Río de la Plata
      one is accounted for by her other three grandparents &mdash; two South African, two British Isles.
      That is what a clean four-grandparent reading looks like, and it makes the Argentine group
      <em>harder</em> to explain away rather than easier. <strong>The thing it proves, it proves more
      firmly than before.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>A second company read the same saliva</h2>
  <p class="dek"><strong>16 September 2026.</strong> Everything above came from one company. The
    MyHeritage kit was uploaded in May 2018 from a <strong>23andMe</strong> account, so the same sample
    has been read twice, by two laboratories, against two different reference panels. That is the nearest
    thing to an independent test that exists without taking a new swab &mdash; and on two points it
    disagrees with what this page has been saying.</p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:20px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Her profile at 23andMe</th><th class="m">%</th><th>Whose it is</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>French &amp; German</strong></td><td class="m">{C["French & German"]}</td>
          <td>the Cape. Huguenot and German, which is what an Afrikaner grandparent pair is made of.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>British &amp; Irish</strong></td><td class="m">{C["British & Irish"]}</td>
          <td>Mary Septima Taylor, and the Cape&rsquo;s British layer. Named down to
            <em>Greater London</em> and <em>County Cork</em>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Broadly Northwestern European</td><td class="m">{C["Broadly Northwestern European"]}</td><td>unresolved northern European.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Italian</strong></td><td class="m">{C["Italian"]}</td>
          <td><strong>nowhere to come from but Pablo Armando</strong> &mdash; and 23andMe names
            <strong>Sicily</strong>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Spanish &amp; Portuguese</strong></td><td class="m">{C["Spanish & Portuguese"]}</td>
          <td>small, but <strong>not zero</strong>, where MyHeritage gave zero.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scandinavian</td><td class="m">{C["Scandinavian"]}</td><td>the Cape again.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Angolan &amp; Congolese</strong></td><td class="m">{C["Angolan & Congolese"]}</td>
          <td rowspan="2"><strong>the Cape, and unambiguously.</strong> A trace of West Central African
            and a trace of South Asian together are the signature of the eighteenth-century Cape &mdash;
            the slave households the Booyzen line comes out of. It belongs to her mother&rsquo;s side and
            it is written up in the <a href="https://daviddef.github.io/TheBooyzen/dna/">Booyzen
            archive</a>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Northern Indian &amp; Pakistani</strong></td><td class="m">{C["Northern Indian & Pakistani"]}</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">23andMe composition version of {tm.ancestry_composition.version_dated}.
    European total {tm.ancestry_composition.european_total}%.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:24px 0">
    <div class="lab">The one thing two companies agree on</div>
    <p class="lede" style="margin:10px 0 0">Italian &mdash; <strong>11.1%</strong> at MyHeritage,
      <strong>8.0%</strong> at 23andMe</p>
    <p style="margin-top:10px">Two laboratories, two reference panels, one signal, in a woman whose other
      three grandparents are English and Cape Afrikaner. <strong>This is now the best-supported thing in
      the whole DNA file</strong> &mdash; better supported than the 0% Iberian it was originally hung on,
      because that figure turned out to be one company&rsquo;s opinion and this one did not.</p>
    <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">At a quarter, {C["Italian"]}% in her implies roughly
      <strong>32%</strong> in him; MyHeritage&rsquo;s figure implies 44%. Somewhere between one Italian
      parent and one Italian grandparent. The error bars on a quarter are large and that range is the
      honest width of them.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note"><strong>And one thing that is behind a paywall.</strong> 23andMe will say
    <em>which parent</em> each slice of ancestry came from &mdash; it is called Parental Inheritance, and
    it does not need a parent to have tested. <strong>It would say outright whether the Italian sits on
    her father&rsquo;s side, where Pablo Armando is, or her mother&rsquo;s, where the Cape is.</strong>
    That is the question this entire page argues around, and one screen would answer it. It requires
    23andMe+ Premium, and <strong>this archive does not buy sources</strong>. It is on
    <a href={u("/worklist/")}>the work list</a> as David&rsquo;s.</div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Eleven thousand cousins, and not one of them is a Lerena</h2>
  <p class="dek">The ethnicity estimates are models. <strong>Matches are people.</strong> On 16 September
    2026 her match lists were read properly for the first time &mdash; <strong>{corpus.corpus.total_matches.toLocaleString()} at
    MyHeritage</strong>, of which <strong>{corpus.corpus.with_a_family_tree.toLocaleString()} have a family
    tree attached</strong>, and <strong>{tm.dna_relatives.total.toLocaleString()} at 23andMe</strong>.
    {corpus.corpus.harvested_here.toLocaleString()} of the MyHeritage matches were pulled down to
    {corpus.corpus.harvest_floor_cm} cM, which covers everything this archive would act on.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:26px">First, the control</h3>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>A null is worth nothing until something known returns in the same form. Four surnames from the
      <em>other</em> three grandparents were run first, and all four came back &mdash; including matches
      for whom the surname appears <strong>only inside their family tree</strong>, never in their own
      name. <strong>The search reaches into trees.</strong> Only then were the nulls believed.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:9rem">Control</th><th class="m">Hits</th><th>What came back</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {controlRows.map((r) => (
          <tr><td><strong>{r.probe}</strong></td><td class="m">{r.hits}</td>
            <td>{r.what_came_back} <span class="cap" set:html={em(r.reading)} /></td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:30px">Then the name itself</h3>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:9rem">Probe</th><th class="m">Hits</th><th>What came back, and what it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {probeRows.map((r) => (
          <tr><td><strong>{r.probe}</strong></td><td class="m">{r.hits}</td>
            <td>{r.what_came_back} <span class="cap" set:html={em(r.reading)} /></td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px"><strong>The sharpest of those is not Lerena. It is
    Lenguas.</strong> The hypothesis on this site reaches for the Montevideo trunk &mdash; Luis
    <strong>Lerena Lenguas</strong>, and the Lerena Traibels beside him. Both compounds are rare. Both
    return <strong>nothing</strong>, in {corpus.corpus.with_a_family_tree.toLocaleString()} trees.
    <a href={u("/hypotheses/")}>The hypotheses &rarr;</a></div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:30px">Where her cousins actually are</h3>
  <p class="dek">23andMe asks every tester to name the countries their grandparents were born in. This is
    a field they filled in themselves, not a guess from where they live now. Across her
    {tm.dna_relatives.total.toLocaleString()} closest relatives, <strong>twenty countries</strong> are
    named.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Country a relative names a grandparent from</th><th class="m">Relatives</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {bpList.map(([cc, n]) => (
          <tr><td>{cc}</td><td class="m">{n}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--ochre,#8a7f5c);margin:22px 0">
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>Not one of the twenty is in South America, and not one is Iberian.</strong>
      No Argentina. No Uruguay. No Spain. <strong>Italy is ninth, with ten</strong> &mdash; five of whom
      name two Italian grandparents, and <strong>one of whom names all four</strong>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The MyHeritage side says the same thing, and says it over the whole corpus rather than a sample.
      MyHeritage publishes its own count of where all <strong>{corpus.corpus.total_matches.toLocaleString()}</strong> of
      her matches live. <strong>Sixty-two countries are named</strong> &mdash; down to a single match
      each in Vietnam, Saint Lucia and the Cayman Islands.</p>
    <div class="grid g2" style="margin:20px 0">
      <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--ochre,#8a7f5c)">
        <div class="nm">{corpus.countries_of_residence.the_ones_that_matter_here.Argentina}</div>
        <div class="txt"><strong>Argentina.</strong> It is not on the list at all.</div></div>
      <div class="panel"><div class="nm">{corpus.countries_of_residence.the_ones_that_matter_here.Uruguay}</div>
        <div class="txt"><strong>Uruguay</strong> &mdash; and those three are the one family read above.
          All three of their kits.</div></div>
    </div>
    <p>Against that: {corpus.countries_of_residence.top.USA.toLocaleString()} in the United States,
      {corpus.countries_of_residence.top["United Kingdom"].toLocaleString()} in the United Kingdom,
      {corpus.countries_of_residence.top["South Africa"].toLocaleString()} in South Africa,
      {corpus.countries_of_residence.top.Australia} in Australia,
      {corpus.countries_of_residence.top.Netherlands} in the Netherlands.</p>
    <p class="cap">Spain shows {corpus.countries_of_residence.the_ones_that_matter_here.Spain} and
      Portugal {corpus.countries_of_residence.the_ones_that_matter_here.Portugal}, which looks like an
      Iberian signal and is not one. <strong>Read the names and they are van Heerden, Retief, Vorster,
      de Vos, Deetlefs, Hanekom</strong> &mdash; South Africans who retired to the Algarve. Country of
      residence is a trap, and this is what it looks like.</p>
    <p>And MyHeritage has computed a documented relationship path &mdash; a Theory of Family Relativity
      &mdash; for <strong>{corpus.relationship_theories.count}</strong> of her matches.
      <strong>Every one of the ten largest resolves to the Cape</strong>: Booyzen, Barry, Kolbe, Kruger,
      Pretorius, Botha, Jordaan, du Toit, De Villiers, Malan. <strong>Not one of them reaches Pablo
      Armando.</strong></p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:30px">The one South American family, read</h3>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>There is exactly one. A <strong>Uruguayan family</strong> &mdash; three kits sharing a single tree
      of <strong>{corpus.the_uruguayan_tree.size} people</strong>. The eldest of the three shares
      <strong>21.4 cM across two segments</strong>, largest 11.3; MyHeritage rates him a
      <strong>fifth cousin at medium confidence</strong>, and reports that the ancestral places common to
      him and her <strong>include Argentina</strong>. They are living and are not named here. Their tree
      was opened and read.</p>
    <p>It is <strong>Basque-Navarrese and French-Basque</strong>, settled in Uruguay:
      {corpus.the_uruguayan_tree.deceased_ancestral_surnames.join(", ")} &mdash; back to
      <em>{corpus.the_uruguayan_tree.oldest_named}</em>. Those people are long dead, which is why they can
      be named here when the living cannot.</p>
    <p><strong>There is no Lerena in it.</strong> No Llerena, no Lenguas, no Traibel, no Aguilar, no
      Fernández. It is a real Río de la Plata connection and <strong>it does not corroborate the
      hypothesis</strong>. At 21 cM on a largest segment of 11 it is also close enough to the noise floor
      that this archive would not build on it either way.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>And then the clusters came back</h2>
  <p class="dek">David generated <strong>MyHeritage AutoClusters</strong> on three kits the same evening.
    The tool groups a person&rsquo;s matches into blocks that all match <em>each other</em> as well as the
    tester &mdash; so each block is, in practice, <strong>one ancestral couple&rsquo;s descendants</strong>.
    It is the sharpest instrument either company offers, and it needs no interpretation from this archive
    to be read.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent-2,#5c8a5c);margin:22px 0">
    <div class="lab">The control &mdash; and it is her son&rsquo;s chart, not hers</div>
    <p style="margin:10px 0 0">{ac.the_control_that_makes_this_readable.what_it_shows}</p>
    <p style="margin-top:10px"><strong>{ac.the_control_that_makes_this_readable.why_it_matters}</strong></p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:26px">Her chart: {ac.the_finding.her_clusters} clusters,
    {ac.the_finding.her_matches_clustered} matches, floor {ac.the_finding.her_cM_floor} cM</h3>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m">Cluster</th><th class="m">Size</th><th class="m">cM</th>
        <th>The families in it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {ac.kits.Cheryl.detail.map((c) => (
          <tr><td class="m">{c.cluster}</td><td class="m">{c.size}</td>
            <td class="m">{c.cM_low}&ndash;{c.cM_high}</td>
            <td>{c.tree_surnames.join(", ")}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--ochre,#8a7f5c);margin:24px 0">
    <p class="lede" style="margin:0">{ac.the_finding.not_one}</p>
    <p style="margin-top:12px"><strong>{ac.the_finding.the_shape_of_it}</strong></p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:26px">The nephew&rsquo;s chart, which nobody expected</h3>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The only other Lerena who has tested produced <strong>{ac.the_nephew.clusters} clusters</strong>
      &mdash; and {ac.the_nephew.surprise}</p>
    <p><strong>He shares exactly {ac.the_nephew.shared_with_his_aunt} clustered matches with his
