<section class="blk">
  <h2>So what does all that do to the case?</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>It pulls in two directions, and both have to be said.</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Stronger:</strong> the Italian. It now rests on two companies rather than on one
      company&rsquo;s zero. That is a different class of evidence from where it stood this morning.</p>
    <p><strong>Weaker:</strong> the Uruguayan trunk. If Pablo Armando descended from Sancho Llerena&rsquo;s
      Montevideo family &mdash; eight children born 1793&ndash;1815, and a visible nineteenth-century
      presence in the city &mdash; then somewhere among 11,581 matches with
      {corpus.corpus.with_a_family_tree.toLocaleString()} trees, and 1,500 more at another company, a
      cousin of that family might be expected to appear. <strong>None does.</strong></p>
    <p><strong>And the clusters put that in its hardest form.</strong> Her fourteen clusters are
      exhaustively Cape and British. Her son&rsquo;s chart, run the same night, throws up
      <strong>two Croatian clusters</strong> for <em>his</em> father &mdash; so the tool plainly does find
      a foreign paternal line when one has tested. <strong>It found his father&rsquo;s. It did not find
      hers.</strong></p>
    <p><strong>And the honest counterweight to that.</strong> Twenty countries of grandparent-birth among
      1,500 relatives, and <em>none</em> of them South American, is not really a fact about this family
      &mdash; it is a fact about <strong>who buys DNA tests</strong>. Uruguay and Argentina are barely
      present in either database. An absence measured in a place where nobody is standing is a weak
      absence, and this page will not pretend otherwise.</p>
    <p><strong>What would actually settle it</strong> is not more searching. It is
      <strong>Parental Inheritance</strong>, which would put the Italian on one side of her or the other;
      and a Rosario baptism for Pablo Armando naming his father. One costs a subscription. The other is
      in a Córdoba parish register that is not filmed.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What it does not do</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>An ethnicity estimate is not evidence of the same class as a record.</strong> At one
      quarter the noise is large, reference panels bleed into one another, and MyHeritage in particular is
      known to distribute Iberian awkwardly across French, North Italian and Sardinian. <strong>A 0%
      Iberian reading is suggestive. It is not a disproof of Spanish ancestry.</strong></p>
    <p>The <strong>genetic group</strong> is much the stronger of the two signals, because it rests on
      shared segments and documented trees rather than on a continental model. And note what it actually
      says: it says <strong>Río de la Plata</strong>. It does not say <em>Italian</em>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What to do with it next</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <ol>
      <li><strong>See the other five genetic groups.</strong> Only three of eight are shown at high
        confidence. If an Italian group sits among the other five, this moves from arithmetic to evidence.
        One slider, one screenshot.</li>
      <li><strong>Work the matches, not the percentages.</strong> A single match with a documented
        Argentine or Italian tree is worth more than any estimate.</li>
      <li><strong>Re-read the sweeps for LERINA.</strong> The Rosario index sweeps recorded no Lerena,
        Llerena, Larena or Lorena &mdash; <strong>Lerina was never separately looked for</strong>, and it
        sorts adjacent.</li>
      <li><strong>CEMLA under LERINA and LARENA</strong>, treated as primary spellings rather than
        variants.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>
